'''Taxis of Burawa''' comprise the national {{w|taxicab}} and {{w|vehicle for hire}} system of [[Burawa]]. Unlike most countries, where taxis are privately operated, all taxis in Burawa are operated by public transit agencies under metropolitan and urban [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|administrations]], with drivers employed as salaried state workers. The system is a supplementary component of Burawa's broader {{w|public transport}} network, providing on-demand point-to-point transport in areas where scheduled transit is less comprehensive or for riders requiring direct service.

As of 2026, approximately 104,500 taxiworkers are employed by administration transit agencies across Burawa, with a median salary ranging from 48,200 to 72,400 [[Hanti]] ($12,050–$18,100). Taxiworkers are represented by the [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa]], the principal union for taxi workers in the country, established in 1979.

The taxi system has undergone significant changes since its formal establishment in 1964, evolving from informal post-revolution vehicle-for-hire arrangements to a standardised, state-operated fleet. The system has faced competition from {{w|ride-hailing}} platforms and, most recently, from the deployment of the [[WhA Locus]] autonomous {{w|robotaxi}}, which triggered nationwide protests by the Taxiworkers Union in 2026.

== History ==

=== Pre-revolution cabs (pre-1932) ===

Prior to the [[Socialist Revolution in Burawa|socialist revolution]] of 1932, privately operated cabs were a common feature of urban transport in Burawa, particularly among the elite and middle class. Cab services were concentrated in major cities such as [[Samara]], [[Mazar]], and [[Kaysoor]], where they served wealthy merchants, colonial administrators, and professionals. The vehicles were typically owned by small operators or fleet companies and were unregulated in terms of vehicle standards, pricing, or driver qualifications.

The socialist revolution of 1932 fundamentally altered the transportation landscape. The nationalisation of essential industries and the establishment of state-operated public transit systems eliminated the economic niche occupied by private cab operators. Most private vehicles were requisitioned or nationalised, and the practice of operating cabs for private hire effectively ceased. The role that cabs had filled — on-demand, point-to-point transport — was assumed by the expanding state public transit system.

=== Informal vehicle-for-hire period (1932–1964) ===

Following the socialist revolution, formal taxi services did not exist for over three decades. The state focused on building comprehensive public transportation networks — buses, trams, and rail — as the primary means of urban and intercity transit. However, gaps in coverage, particularly in smaller cities and rural areas, created demand for informal vehicle-for-hire arrangements.

During this period, informal hiring of vehicles for hire was practised on a small scale, typically through personal networks or ad hoc arrangements at markets, transit hubs, and community gatherings. These arrangements were technically unregulated but generally tolerated, as they filled a gap in the state's transportation network. The informal nature of these services meant that vehicle quality, pricing, and driver reliability varied widely, and there was no formal mechanism for consumer protection or labour organisation.

=== Vehicle Hire Act of 1964 ===

The [[Vehicle Hire Act of 1964]] was passed by the [[Parliament of Burawa]] as part of the post-[[Great Adonian War]] programme of economic and social reform. The Act authorised metropolitan and urban administrations to establish and operate vehicle-for-hire services as a supplement to scheduled public transportation. The legislation framed taxi services as a public good — an extension of the state's transportation responsibilities — rather than a commercial activity.

The Act granted administrations the authority to acquire vehicles, employ drivers, set fares, and designate service areas. Taxi services were to be integrated into the existing public transit framework, with fares structured to be affordable while covering operational costs. The Act did not mandate the establishment of taxi services in all administrations; rather, it permitted each administration to determine whether and how to implement vehicle-for-hire services based on local demand and resources.

Initial adoption was slow. Most major metropolitan administrations already had comprehensive bus and rail networks, and the demand for supplementary taxi service was limited. The administrations that first established taxi services were those where public transportation was less comprehensive or where specific local conditions favoured vehicle-for-hire services:

* [[Lanwaley]] and [[Sago]] — cities where the public transit network was less developed than in the major metropolitan corridors, and where taxi services filled meaningful gaps in coverage
* [[Mazar]] — where taxi use acquired a {{w|status symbol}} dimension, as Mazar was the only city in Burawa with private property and a significant private-sector economy; taxi ridership was associated with professional and commercial elites who preferred the privacy and convenience of individual transport

In the first decade after the Act, the number of taxiworkers nationwide remained modest, growing from approximately 3,000 in 1964 to roughly 15,000 by the early 1970s.

=== Scam and kidnapping crisis (1970s) ===

During the early years of state taxi services, vehicles used for hire were not standardised. Administrations deployed whatever vehicles were available in their fleets, and a taxi was distinguished only by a removable taxi marker placed on the roof of the vehicle. This practice created a significant vulnerability: private individuals could place taxi markers on unauthorised vehicles and impersonate state taxi drivers, picking up passengers at taxi stands and transit hubs.

By the early 1970s, this vulnerability had been exploited in a series of widely publicised scams and kidnappings. Criminals using false taxi markers would collect passengers — particularly tourists and visitors unfamiliar with local conditions — and then rob, defraud, or abduct them. The absence of vehicle standardisation meant that passengers had no reliable means of distinguishing legitimate state taxis from impostor vehicles. The crisis intensified through the mid-1970s, with incidents reported in multiple cities and growing public concern about the safety of the taxi system.

=== Taxicab Standardization Bill of 1977 ===

In response to the scam and kidnapping crisis, the [[Taxicab Standardization Bill of 1977]] was passed by Parliament. The legislation mandated that all state-operated taxis be drawn from a restricted list of approved vehicle models and be painted in a standardised livery. The Act designated two exclusive vehicle models for taxi service:

* The [[BMC 18]] — a {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}} manufactured by the [[Burawi Motor Company]] (BMC), used for standard taxi service
* The [[BMC MiniBus 4]] — a {{w|minivan}} manufactured by BMC, used for larger groups and accessibility-compatible service

The Act established a five-year review cycle, authorising the [[Ministry of Transportation (Burawa)|Ministry of Transportation]] to update vehicle standards periodically. Subsequent review cycles replaced earlier models with newer BMC vehicles as they became available.

The standardised livery was a {{w|lime (color)|lime}} green and white {{w|checkered pattern|checkered}} colour scheme that became strongly associated with Burawi taxi service in the late 1970s and 1980s. The livery was designed to be immediately distinguishable from private vehicles, making it difficult for criminals to impersonate state taxis. The checkered pattern, combined with the restricted vehicle models, provided a visual guarantee of authenticity that the previous removable-marker system had lacked.

The standardisation programme was implemented progressively, with administrations replacing their existing taxi fleets with BMC 18 and BMC MiniBus 4 vehicles over the late 1970s and early 1980s. The scam and kidnapping incidents declined sharply following the introduction of standardised vehicles and livery.

=== Taxiworkers Union of Burawa (1979) ===

{{main|Taxiworkers Union of Burawa}}

The [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa]] was established in 1979 as the nationwide union representing taxiworkers employed by administration transit agencies. The union's initial organising campaign was driven primarily by chronic undersupply of taxiworkers. Demand for taxi services had grown faster than administrations were hiring drivers, leading to long wait times and limited availability, particularly during peak hours and in smaller cities. The union's first major campaign petitioned administrations to increase hiring and expand taxi fleets.

The union also advocated for standardised working conditions across administrations, as salary, benefits, and scheduling practices varied between metropolitan and urban agencies. The establishment of the union marked the formal recognition of taxiwork as a distinct profession within the Burawi public-sector workforce.

=== Decline and tourism revival (1990s) ===

During the 1990s, taxi ridership in Burawa declined as the state continued to invest in and expand its public transportation systems. New bus routes, extended rail lines, and improved service frequency reduced the need for supplementary taxi service in many areas. The number of taxiworkers contracted modestly during this period.

However, the growth of [[tourism in Burawa|tourism]] during the 1990s reversed the decline in several cities. Burawa's public transit system required passengers to use {{w|fare card}} systems that were complex for visitors to navigate, involving multi-zone pricing, transfer rules, and region-specific card types. Many tourists opted for taxi service as a simpler alternative, particularly in cities with high tourist traffic. The tourism-driven demand led administrations in tourist destinations to increase hiring of taxiworkers, and the total number of taxiworkers rose during the late 1990s.

=== Fare card standardisation and rideshare competition (2000s) ===

In the 2000s, two developments reduced taxi demand. First, the {{w|fare card}} systems used by Burawa's public transit agencies were progressively standardised across regions, making it easier for occasional users and tourists to navigate the system without resorting to taxis. The introduction of unified fare media and improved information availability — including the growth of online resources about Burawa's transit systems — reduced the complexity that had driven tourists to taxis.

Second, the rise of {{w|ride-hailing}} applications — most notably [[Sare]] (through its [[Sare Socdo]] service, launched in 2012) and [[Z2Z]], a domestic startup — introduced a new form of competition. While private vehicles for hire that were not operated by licensed state cabs were not illegal in Burawa, the practice had been virtually unheard of prior to the emergence of ride-hailing apps. The apps provided a digital platform that connected passengers with drivers, including those using private vehicles, bypassing the state taxi system entirely.

The combination of fare card standardisation and ride-hailing competition led to a renewed slump in taxi ridership and hiring during the 2000s and early 2010s.

=== 2016 protest and Licensed Ride Hailing Act ===

{{main|2016 Taxiworkers protest (Burawa)|Licensed Ride Hailing Act}}

In 2016, the [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa]] organised the first major taxiworkers protest in response to the decline in taxi hiring caused by the growth of ride-hailing applications. The protest highlighted the competitive pressure that private ride-hailing platforms placed on state-operated taxi services, which were required to adhere to public-sector employment standards, fare regulations, and vehicle requirements that private platforms did not face.

The government of [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]] [[Marik Sahamin]] responded by enacting the [[Licensed Ride Hailing Act]], which prohibited the operation of private vehicles for hire that were not run by the state when orchestrated by a third party. The Act effectively banned ride-hailing platforms from using private, non-state-licensed vehicles, requiring that all ride-hailing dispatch be conducted through state-licensed taxi fleets. The government cited safety concerns, noting reports of {{w|sexual assault}} incidents involving ride-hailing drivers in Burawa and abroad as justification for the restriction.

The legislation was controversial. Supporters argued that it protected passenger safety and preserved the employment standards of the state taxi system. Critics, including [[Sare]] and [[Z2Z]], argued that it restricted consumer choice and protected a state monopoly from competition. Following the Act's passage, ride-hailing platforms in Burawa were required to partner with state transit agencies and use licensed taxi drivers for ride-hailing services.

=== BuFulid (2018) ===

In 2018, the [[Ministry of Innovation (Burawa)|Ministry of Innovation]] (WhA) developed [[BuFulid]], a state-operated {{w|ride-hailing}} application for taxi drivers, providing a digital dispatch platform for the state taxi fleet. BuFulid was designed to offer the convenience of ride-hailing — on-demand booking, GPS tracking, estimated arrival times, and cashless payment — while operating exclusively with state-licensed taxiworkers. The app was part of the government's broader strategy to modernise state services and provide a competitive digital alternative to private ride-hailing platforms.

=== WhA Locus deployment and 2026 protests ===

{{main|WhA Locus|2026 Taxiworkers Union protest|April 2026 Taxiworkers Union protest}}

On 14 August 2026, the [[WhA Locus]] — a battery electric autonomous {{w|robotaxi}} developed by the [[Ministry of Innovation (Burawa)|Ministry of Innovation]] in collaboration with the [[Burawi Motor Company]] — entered service with the South Hudur Metro Transportation Authority in the [[South Hudur Metropolitan Administration]], with an initial deployment of 150 vehicles. The deployment triggered a nationwide work stoppage by the [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa]], which objected to the introduction of autonomous vehicles in taxi service and the potential displacement of human drivers.

The protest followed an earlier demonstration on 18 April 2026, when the union organised a three-day work stoppage in response to the commencement of Locus testing in [[Khar]]. The [[Transportation Workers Displacement Fund]], established in 2022 to provide salary and pension support for transportation workers facing legislated automation — initially covering passenger rail operators facing a 2035 phase-out — did not include taxiworkers among its eligible categories. A legislative attempt to extend the fund to taxiworkers, the [[Transportation Workers Displacement Fund Addendum]], passed the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] on 11 August 2026 but failed in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] on 12 August.

