== Operations ==

=== Employment structure ===

All taxiworkers in Burawa are employed as salaried state workers by metropolitan and urban administration transit agencies. Drivers are not independent contractors and do not own their vehicles; vehicles are assigned from the administration's fleet. Employment includes standard public-sector benefits, including healthcare, pension contributions, and paid leave.

The median salary for taxiworkers ranges from 48,200 to 72,400 [[Hanti]] ($12,050–$18,100), varying by administration and region. Salaries in major metropolitan administrations are generally higher than in smaller urban administrations, reflecting differences in cost of living and fare revenue.

As of 2026, approximately 104,500 taxiworkers are employed nationwide. The largest concentrations are in metropolitan administrations with significant tourism traffic or less comprehensive public transit coverage, including the [[South Hudur Metropolitan Administration]], [[Sago]]-[[Dare]] metropolitan area, and [[Greater Lanwaley]].

=== Vehicles ===

Since the [[Taxicab Standardization Bill of 1977]], the [[Ministry of Transportation (Burawa)|Ministry of Transportation]] has been authorised to set vehicle standards for state-operated taxis on a five-year review cycle. The original 1977 standardisation designated the [[BMC 18]] {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}} and [[BMC MiniBus 4]] {{w|minivan}} as the sole permitted taxi vehicles. Subsequent review cycles replaced earlier models with newer BMC vehicles as they became available.

The current taxi fleet, as of 2026, consists of four vehicle models manufactured by the [[Burawi Motor Company]] (BMC):

* [[BMC Wiyar]] (2016) — a {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}} used for standard taxi service
* [[BMC Wiyar]] (2018) — an electric {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}}, introduced following the [[Smart Transportation Act of 2016]]
* [[BMC Badalga]] (2018) — a {{w|minivan}} used for larger groups, accessibility-compatible service, and higher-capacity routes
* [[BMC Badalga]] (2022) — an electric {{w|minivan}}, introduced as part of the ongoing fleet electrification programme

The vehicles are painted in the standardised {{w|lime (color)|lime}} green and white {{w|checkered pattern|checkered}} livery introduced in 1977. The livery has become an iconic visual element of Burawi urban life, particularly associated with the late 1970s and 1980s.

The [[Smart Transportation Act of 2016]] mandated that all new public transit vehicles purchased by the state must be electric. As existing internal combustion vehicles reach the end of their service life, they are being replaced with electric equivalents. The [[WhA Locus]], a purpose-built electric autonomous taxi, represents the next generation of the state taxi fleet, though its deployment has been the subject of significant labour controversy.

=== Fares and payment ===

Taxi fares in Burawa are {{w|taximeter|metered}} and set by the operating administration transit agency. Each administration establishes its own fare structure, which is reviewed periodically and adjusted for inflation, fuel costs, and operational expenses. Fares are calculated using a {{w|taximeter}} combining a base flag-drop charge, a per-kilometre distance rate, and a per-minute waiting time rate. The fare structure is designed to be affordable while covering operational costs, consistent with the system's role as a supplementary public service.

